xnu: 0089 sweet panic flush + task ledger beats

drill: 0089 sweet force the panic string onto the venus screen and
beat init_task_ledgers

Boot 18 on venus (commit d45b0f3, run 39290866805) wakes past every
0085/0086/0087 drill ("probe sentinel restored DAIF"), walks through
Telemetry/Scheduler/BANK/IPC and then stops right after

    debug_log_init: Error!! gPanicBase is still not initialized
    panic_trace: PE_arm_debug_enable_trace enter
    panic_trace: PE_arm_debug_enable_trace exit

with a silent freeze (ARIAS photos).  QEMU with the same 0088 kernel
prints the next event: panic(cpu 0 caller ...): couldn't create entries
for task ledger template @task.c:1265 (init_task_ledgers).  Source
reading explains the difference as a swallow, not a platform wedge:

- gPanicBase is only initialized by check_for_panic_log()
  (pexpert/arm/pe_init.c) and sweet ADTs carry no iBoot "pram" node and
  no embedded-panic-log-size: gPanicBase stays 0 for this whole boot.
- debug_log_init() therefore errors out and the debug_buf pointers stay
  NULL; every paniclog_append_noflush() call is dropped.
- panic() does have a sweet framebuffer hook from patch 0010
  (osfmk/kern/debug.c, panic_trap_to_debugger), but it routes through
  sweet_early_panic(), which returns as soon as the early console was
  handed over to the serial console.  On sweet the fbcon console
  registers itself as a serial device, so serial_init() returns 1 and
  the hand-over (early_disabled=1) happens on every venus boot: the
  panic string was swallowed as well.
- the debugger tail print (panic_debugger_log at the end of
  debugger_collect_diagnostics) never ran on hardware because
  PEHaltRestartInternal(kPEPanicBegin) -> PE_init_panicheader() hits its
  assert(gPanicBase != 0); the nested panic that follows degrades to the
  nested-panic stall in panic_stop()/panic_spin_forever() with all of
  its messages dropped into the paniclog that does not exist.  That is
  the ARIAS freeze point.  (QEMU printed the panic line earlier in the
  path because it crossed debugger_collect_diagnostics while console
  output was still linked in.)

This patch, everything inside #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
(unless already gated by an existing #if block):

- SWEET.h: replace PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_DISABLED with a real panic log
 region: PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_PADDR 0xaff80000, PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG_SIZE
  0x80000 - the tail of the ABL ramdump-framebuffer reservation
  (0xaf000000..0xb0000000), unused after the splash, below the kernel
  physBase (0xb0000000) so no later VM allocation can take it.  Same
  emergency hatch BCM2837.h uses (0x3c0fc000).  check_for_panic_log()
  then takes the PLATFORM_PANIC_LOG branch: gPanicBase is ready
  extremely early, PE_init_panicheader() drops the assert with it and
  debug_log_init() ends "OK.", so from now on every panic has a real
  paniclog buffer.
- pe_init.c: print the mapped panic log region once ("P0089: panic
  log mapped ...") for boot log confirmation.
- debug.c: the panic_trap_to_debugger hook now calls a new
  sweet_early_panic_flush(fmt, args, caller), which ignores the
  early_console hand-over and prints "P0089: panic flush: <string>
  caller 0x..." and "P0089: panic out force-flush" straight into the
  framebuffer before any panic machinery can stall, and restores the
  previous early_disabled state.  So even among future panic stalls the
  inputs of the panic are on screen, at the very front of the chain.
- sweet_early.c/h: the flush implementation (no locks, no allocation,
  _doprnt only, as sweet_early_panic before).
- task.c: init_task_ledgers() position beats: template created, one
  named beat per main ledger group with the value printed, SFI class
  beats in addition, "all entries ok" before template_complete, and the
  (dead-with-coalitions) task_init-init_task_ledgers branch beat.  If
  venus really dies in init_task_ledgers, the last P0089 line names the
  ledger group that the entries stopped at.
- coalition.c: "P0089: coalitions_init: calling init_task_ledgers"
  before the call this boot stream runs (CONFIG_COALITIONS is on).

Verify on next boot: panic log mapping row + 'panic out force-flush'
with the actual panic story, or the ledger beats pinning the failing
group.
